Distinguish concentration correlations and uncertainty in an observation (@physics-statistical-fluctuations: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Use-controlled-infinite-sums-in-a-stipulated-energy-ladder.lingua#r_0020301215067F6BEA7F416700") { r_00BB3DEB755830AE5E94CAF4BA
In the independent canonical binary model J is a sum of a indicators, each with mean p and variance p(1−p). Independence removes cross covariances, giving Var(J)=ap(1−p). For the fraction J/a with a>0, variance is p(1−p)/a. Its standard deviation decreases as 1/√a at fixed p. The absolute count's standard deviation grows as √a; these statements are compatible. Relative standard deviation of J is √[(1−p)/(ap)] when p>0, so a large a alone is insufficient near a very rare excitation.

A direct concentration bound needs no Gaussian approximation. On the event |X−E[X]|≥d>0 the nonnegative squared deviation is at least d², so Var(X)≥d²P(|X−E[X]|≥d). Applied to J/a this gives a tail bound p(1−p)/(a d²), capped at one. It is a bound for the stipulated ensemble, not a confidence interval for an unknown parameter inferred from observed data. Reuse [Describe a chance quantity by its weighted mean and spread](../../inquiry/core/Describe-a-chance-quantity-by-its-weighted-mean-and-spread.lingua#r_0A9487B256CD8C0ED0083290DA) for the distinction between model variance and estimated variation.

Correlations can change the scaling completely. If every site equals the same random binary variable Y, then J=aY and Var(J)=a²p(1−p); the fraction does not concentrate. In the fixed-total model, negative correlations instead give Var(J)=ap(1−p)(N−a)/(N−1). For a=3 and p=3/8, the ratio of fixed-total to independent variance is (N−3)/(N−1), tending to one at fixed a as N grows. For a=N, the fixed-total variance stays zero. A statement about small local subsystems cannot be promoted to a statement about every extensive observable.

An observation also has a measurement procedure: which quantity, sampling times, resolution, calibration and perturbation of the system. A physical fluctuation in energy differs from sensor noise, uncertain calibration or uncertainty in a fitted temperature. Two distributions with the same mean energy may be separated by a variance or a joint observation, but a finite empirical variance also has sampling uncertainty. No set of exact model checks supplies an apparatus validation or an actual dataset.

For an energy spectrum with finitely many fixed levels and fixed finite a, Z is a finite sum of smooth positive exponentials for finite T>0. Thermodynamic functions derived from ln Z are smooth there. A sharp macroscopic phase-transition singularity requires a suitable limiting argument or a changed model; a rounded maximum in a small system's response is not that argument. Interactions, collective order and phase coexistence remain subsequent formation.

**Work:** prove the variance scaling and tail bound. Compare independent, perfectly correlated and fixed-total preparations with the same one-site mean. Choose a discriminating observable and describe how measurement noise and correlated sampling could obscure it. Derive a finite-size bound before using an informal thermodynamic-limit statement.

**Continue:** [Use controlled infinite sums in a stipulated energy ladder](Use-controlled-infinite-sums-in-a-stipulated-energy-ladder.lingua#r_0020301215067F6BEA7F416700).

